  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` rejected prices with thousands separators, such as `\"2,450.50\"` and `\"1,299.00\"`. Its regex failed to match, `match()` returned `null`, and the next line threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. That surfaced as a 500. I checked the log: all 37 of the `POST /checkout` 500s came from orders with comma-grouped prices.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- It now accepts comma-grouped prices (`1,299.00` and the like).\n- Input that isn't an amount now throws the documented `Invalid amount: <input>` error instead of a `TypeError`.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):** I added one regression test covering `2,450.50`, `1,299.00` and a full checkout with a comma-grouped price. I added a second test that invalid input throws the descriptive error. `npm test` passes (6 of 6).\n\nI didn't change how the server turns `Invalid amount` errors into responses. Genuinely bad input may still produce a 500 rather than a 400, depending on `src/server.js`. I didn't open that file.",
